FINDINGS:
1. [MAJOR] tests/doc_blocks_runs.txt:65 — The uncommitted SPEC edit adds two net lines (`SPEC.md:1338-1342`, the new Windows drive-relative sentence) *before* every SPEC block anchored from `SPEC.md:1370` onward, but no uncommitted change re-anchors `tests/doc_blocks_runs.txt`, `doc_blocks_fragments.txt`, or `doc_blocks_known_fail.txt`. The harness keys blocks by the current opening-fence line (`tests/doc_blocks_test.rs:92-94`) and matches rows by `doc:line` (`tests/doc_blocks_test.rs:14`), so those rows now point at prose rather than a fence and the later blocks become unclassified. This contradicts the REPORT's "2413 passed" gate claim. Note the committed anchors also look suspicious: the committed SPEC inserts ~33 lines yet the committed data-file anchors move by only +2, so even HEAD may be mis-anchored. Fix: regenerate/re-anchor all three data files against the working tree (or fold the new sentence into existing lines), then actually run `tests/doc_blocks_test.rs`. VERIFIED: traced the insertion location and the exact-key match; not executed, and the +33-vs-+2 anomaly is what would settle whether HEAD or only the working tree is wrong.

2. [MAJOR] src/path.rs:280-287 — The `#[cfg(target_arch = "wasm32")]` `resolve` refusal (the `IoError` that "names the platform") has no test. `cargo test --all-targets` on the host compiles only the non-wasm arm, and the REPORT's wasm test list covers only directory listing. The playground behavior that SPEC.md now promises is therefore unpinned and could fail to compile on wasm without any gate noticing. Fix: add a wasm-target test (or a cfg-independent helper asserted natively) for the refusal text/kind, plus a wasm compile gate.

3. [MINOR] src/vfs.rs:279 — The in-memory `read_dir` splits the first path component on both `'/'` and `'\\'` on every target. On wasm the separator is `'/'` and `path.join` deliberately does not fold backslashes there (`src/path.rs:98-102`), so a file written as `"a\\b.txt"` is listed as `"a"` instead of `"a\\b.txt"`. Fix: split only on the platform separator (or `MAIN_SEPARATOR`) so a backslash remains an ordinary filename character on wasm, matching the module's own documented POSIX rule.

REVIEW VERDICT: FINDINGS 3
